{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["<b>And you shall make of it a holy anointing oil, a compounded perfume, after the art of the perfumer</b> — On the level of peshat, the preparation of the oil was according to the words of Rabbi Yehudah, who said (Keritot 5a) that they steep them in water so that they would not absorb the oil. The steeping was not that the spices alone would be in water; rather, they placed the crushed spices in a vessel full of water, and floated a hin of olive oil on top of them. Afterward they placed the vessel over the mouth of another vessel full of water, and set it on a gentle fire of embers, and boiled it until the water was dried up, and they collected the oil from above it. For such is the practice of perfumers for every fragrant oil. Therefore Scripture was brief and commanded that they make this oil a compounded perfume, after the manner of the perfumer's art, without specifying the procedure, since the way of perfumers is well known among them. I likewise found in the Jerusalem Talmud in Shekalim (6:1): Rabbi Yehudah says: he would boil them in water and place oil on top; once the fragrance was absorbed, he would skim it off, in the manner that perfumers do, as it is said: \"And you shall make of it a holy anointing oil, a compounded perfume, after the art of the perfumer.\""], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:19:10.324347834Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}